Redproof-Patch: own-canary-target-unprobed
Redproof-Family: ownership
Redproof-Run: TestEveryManagedTargetHasALiveCanaryOrAnAdmissibleExcuse
Redproof-Package: ./internal/canary
Redproof-Expect: have no live canary probe
Redproof-Summary: A managed service target nothing probes — mail-smtp's `smtp` target flipped from self-hosted to managed, which is exactly the shape a newly added managed target has before anyone writes its row. Planted in the registry rather than in the suite on purpose: the guard's population is derived from the manifests, so this proof tests the derivation as well as the refusal, and it depends on no particular adapter package surviving a refactor. It replaces own-canary-adapter-unexcused, whose plant deleted the feature-flags-launchdarkly allowlist entry — a proof that died with its subject when that target was retired, and which proved the weaker rule anyway: under the either/or default a stated reason excused a target, so the deleted-excuse plant only showed that SOMETHING had to be said. The rule now is that the probe is required and the excuse is the narrow exception; the excuse side's own red proofs live in the suite as TestLiveCanaryRefusesAnExemptionWithoutItsEvidence.
